WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.740
Now we are moving to generators.

00:00:02.370 --> 00:00:06.820
Generators are important because they allow Python to produce values one at a time.

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:09.440
Let's compare that with a regular list.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:15.420
If we create a list containing one million transformed values, Python normally creates

00:00:15.270 --> 00:00:18.220
and stores all one million values in memory.

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.540
A generator can work differently.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:25.820
It can calculate a value when we need it, return that value, and then continue later.

00:00:26.330 --> 00:00:28.840
The key word that makes this possible is yield.

00:00:28.690 --> 00:00:31.060
Look at the square's gen function.

00:00:31.870 --> 00:00:33.660
We loop through the numbers up to n.

00:00:34.450 --> 00:00:36.700
Instead of using return, we use yield.

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:41.600
When Python reaches yield, it gives us one value and pauses the function.

00:00:42.110 --> 00:00:46.940
The next time Python asks the generator for another value, the function continues from

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:47.540
where it stopped.

00:00:47.910 --> 00:00:48.740
Run the example.

00:00:49.270 --> 00:00:51.840
You will see the squared values printed one at a time.

00:00:52.450 --> 00:00:56.260
For four values, memory is obviously not a major concern.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:01:01.300
But imagine processing millions of records or reading a very large file.

00:01:01.810 --> 00:01:06.680
Generators can be useful because we do not always need to store every result at the same

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:11.700
time. The important idea for now is that return usually finishes

00:01:11.550 --> 00:01:16.580
a function, while yield produces a value and allows the function to continue later.
